  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 219 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows a cool front stretching across \nlower Michigan to NW Indiana, Central Illinois and northern MO. \nGOES19 shows clear skies across the state and along the front. Dew \npoints remained around 50. Aloft, northwest flow was in place, with \nonly some passing Pacific moisture in place. No precipitation was \nfound with the front.\n\nTonight...\n\nModels suggest the cold front will sag southward across Indiana this \nevening, allowing the winds to shift to the northwest. No \nprecipitation will be expected as this will be a dry front, and \nbarely any clouds present. Thus look for mostly clear skies, with \nlows around 50.\n\nThursday and Friday... Aloft, persistent NW flow aloft on Thursday \nis expected to modify into a building ridge axis across Indiana and \nthe Great Lakes on Friday. Both of these things will continue to \nresult in subsidence across central Indiana. Meanwhile at the \nsurface, strong Canadian high pressure will settle across Indiana \nfrom the northern plains before moving to the middle Atlantic states \nby Friday. This will result in continued dry weather with plenty of \ndaytime sunshine and overall pleasant weather. Highs on Thursday \nshould reach the upper 70s due to mild cold air advection. Warmer \ntemperatures in the 80s will arrive on Friday as southerly flow \nreturns due to the surface high to the east.\n\nSaturday through Wednesday...\n\nWet weather will be expected to arrive on Saturday night as remnants \nfrom a tropical system are suggested to advance to and pass across \nCentral Indiana through Sunday. Forecast soundings suggest pwats \naround 1.5 inches, plenty moist. Thus high pops will be used during \nthis time. The pressure gradient at this time does not to be overly \nstrong, so strong winds are not expected, but wind gusts around 25 \nmph cannot be ruled out.\n\nMonday...\n\nHurricane remnants look to exit on Sunday night into Monday, \nallowing for dry weather to return to start the next work week. \nForecast soundings trend to a dry air mass as surface ridging builds \nacross the area from the deep south. Southwesterly lower level flow \nwill remain in place, keeping temperatures above normal.\n\nTuesday...\n\nA quick moving cold front is expected to pass across Indiana on \nTuesday from the upper midwest. The day should begin within the warm \nsector, but by afternoon the front will have passed and winds will \nbecome northwesterly with strong cold air advection suggested. \nForecast soundings show limited instability and fail to saturate the \ncolumn. However given the strong change in airmass, at least some \npops will need to be included for now. Dry weather will arrive by \nTuesday night as Canadian high pressure builds across Indiana. The \ncore of the cold air will be well to the northeast, 850mb temps will \nfall to around 8C, allowing for more seasonal temperatures on \nWednesday.",
